Material Symbols — SVG path subsets for `icons`
================================================================================

Included files
    tools/material_symbols/*.svg                 (the vendored upstream outlines, one per icon)
    tools/material_symbols/LICENSE               (the upstream licence copy)
    tools/material_symbols/UPSTREAM_HAS_NO_NOTICE (a probe result, see below)
    src/widget/icon_data.rs                      (the generated table)
    src/widget/icon_fallback_data.rs             (the generated fallback geometry)

Origin
    Material Symbols, by Google LLC.
    Project:  https://github.com/google/material-design-icons
    Pin:      commit bd8cb85bd4bad964fe6918f79665bb40c3a8efef (master @ 2026-09-25)
    Style:    symbols/web/<name>/materialsymbolsoutlined/<name>_24px.svg
    SHA-256 of the upstream LICENSE at the pinned commit:
        58d1e17ffe5109a7ae296caafcadfdbe6a7d176f0bc4ab01e12a689b0499d8bd

    The files are vendored, never hand-fetched. Refresh with:
        python3 tools/vendor_material_symbols.py --refresh

What it is
    The **SVG path data** (`d`) of one outline per icon this crate names — not the
    icon font, and not a rendering of it. Each `d` is copied **verbatim**: no outline
    was redrawn, simplified, re-encoded or otherwise altered, so the shipped geometry
    is upstream's to the byte. `src/widget/icon_data.rs` is generated from the
    vendored files and is never hand-edited:
        python3 tools/gen_icon_data.py            # write the table
        python3 tools/gen_icon_data.py --check    # verify it is current

    The data is behind the `icons` feature, which is **on by default**: a plain
    `cargo build` gets these outlines. It is deliberately absent from every device
    profile, because `mini` and `embedded` are sized builds where a payload the caller
    did not ask for is wrong; a profile build that wants icons says
    `--features mini,icons`. A build without the feature draws `src/widget/icon_fallback_data.rs`,
    which is a coarse flattening of the **same** `d` strings — derived, not copied — so
    the fallback and the data cannot describe different shapes for one icon. The
    fallback table is generated by the same command as the data table and is compiled
    on every build (it is what a build without `icons` draws).

